# Cox static-analysis rules.
#
# Companion to .clang-format: clang-format governs *layout* (whitespace, line
# breaks, column limit); clang-tidy governs *structure* (constructs that
# compile but invite bugs). The two are complementary -- clang-format cannot
# add braces, clang-tidy does not reflow code -- and neither is expected to
# enforce the other's rules.
#
# Like .clang-format, this makes a style claim mechanically checkable rather
# than aspirational. See .github/workflows/ci.yml (the "clang-tidy" job).
#
# TOOLING REQUIREMENTS
#   * clang-tidy >= 15 (CI uses 18 on Ubuntu 24.04).
#   * A compile database. Every CMake preset sets
#     CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON. Use the 'examples' preset: it builds the
#     library, tests AND examples, so `cmake --preset examples` produces a
#     build/examples/compile_commands.json that covers ALL first-party code.
#     clang-tidy must be run with `-p build/examples` AND only over files that
#     database actually contains -- platform-specific sources such as
#     reactor/poller/kqueue_poller.cc (macOS/BSD, never built on the Linux CI)
#     are NOT in it, and linting them yields spurious "file not found" /
#     "undeclared identifier" errors. The CI job derives its file list from the
#     database for exactly this reason; do the same locally.
#
# SCOPE (deliberately minimal)
#   One check is enabled: readability-braces-around-statements, which requires
#   braces on every if / else / for / while / do body, including single-
#   statement bodies. Rationale: a brace-less body governs only the next
#   statement, so adding a second line silently moves it outside the block --
#   the shape of Apple's 2014 "goto fail" TLS defect. Google C++ permits
#   brace-less bodies; Cox does not, because the failure mode is security-
#   relevant and invisible in review.
#
#   This composes cleanly with .clang-format's
#   `AllowShortIfStatementsOnASingleLine: WithoutElse`: that option only decides
#   whether an already-braced short `if` may share one line; it never removes
#   braces, so the two tools never disagree.
#
# MAINTENANCE
#   Apply fixes in place after a large merge (over DB files only):
#     clang-tidy -p build/examples --fix \
#       $(sed -n 's/.*"file": "\(.*\)".*/\1/p' \
#            build/examples/compile_commands.json | grep -v /third_party/)
#   To add a check, append it to `Checks`, make the tree clean, then land the
#   config and the fixes together so CI stays green. Prefer enabling one family
#   at a time (bugprone-*, performance-*) rather than a broad wildcard, so each
#   addition is a reviewable, justified decision.

---
# Leading '-*' disables all defaults so the enabled set is explicit and
# auditable; only the checks listed afterwards run.
Checks: >
  -*,
  readability-braces-around-statements

# Promote only the sytle checks we deliberately enable to errors. This is
# intentionally NOT '*': a bare '*' would also promote clang-diagnostic-*
# (compile/setup problems, e.g. a header missing because a file is absent from
# the compile database) to hard failures, making the linter fail for reasons
# unrelated to code style. Scoping to readability-* keeps failures meaningful.
WarningsAsErrors: 'readability-*'

# Analyze Cox's own headers (definitions live in .cc, but inline/templated code
# lives in headers), but NOT vendored headers: a first-party test that includes
# gtest/gtest.h must not be failed by diagnostics originating inside googletest.
# The regex matches any path that is not under a third_party/ directory
# (negative lookahead; verified supported by clang-tidy 18's regex engine -- if
# you must support clang-tidy < 14, replace with an explicit allowlist).
HeaderFilterRegex: '^(?!.*/third_party/).*$'

# When a fix inserts braces, reformat the touched region with the project's
# .clang-format rules so --fix output needs no manual cleanup.
FormatStyle: file
